Z||000000||Heisenhund||05-04-2001||07:14 AM||||Hi all,<P>Spent a lot of time yesterday under my car replacing radiator hoses in a<BR>last ditch effort to find out why my 86 GT gets too hot. After refilling it,<BR>I decided to try to put the last bit of tightness back in the belt. It<BR>wasn't squealing, but I've never felt like it was tight enough. One of the<BR>couple times I dropped the wrench, it happened to bounce over to the<BR>driver's side a bit forward of the pipe drain plug. While I was cursing and<BR>reaching for the wrench, I noticed that the pipe was a bit more deformed<BR>than I thought looked normal. I finished up with what I was doing & decided<BR>to compare the pipe to the one on my 85 GT. Definitely not normal<BR>deformation.<P>My question is this:<BR>How much deformation is enough to restrict flow to the point of causing<BR>cooling problems? I realize that if Pontiac had meant for there to be a<BR>small dent there, they would have installed one but is this bad enough to<BR>warrant searching for new pipes?<P>Thanks for any opinions, views, etc.<P>Heis||||reg||9
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||05-04-2001||09:33 AM||||If sounds like the pipe is kinked? Can you cut the bad part out and put a hose over it?<P>  If sounds like your magic bouncing wrench found what you were looking for. How much do you want for that wrench?<P>  I would say if the pipe has been crushed more than 20% it would most likely explain your cooling problem.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||05-04-2001||08:49 PM||||It's hard to say w/o seeing it. On a V6 it probably won't take much. Inspect the lines from end to end and see if they've been whacked anyplace else as well.||||reg||1
